{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011vsM72JRKUfj8xqcDh7mge","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.85.2)\n2\t\n3\tEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\n4\tfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n5\t\n6\t**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\n7\tis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\n8\tare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\n9\tlacks.\n10\t\n11\t## Start here\n12\t\n13\t1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n14\t   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n15\t2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n16\t   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n17\t   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n18\t   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n19\t   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n20\t   `game/` and make it yours.\n21\t3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n22\t   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n23\t4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n24\t5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n25\t   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n26\t   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n27\t6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n28\t   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n29\t   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n30\t   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n31\t   before you use it.\n32\t\n33\t## What is here\n34\t\n35\t| File | What it is |\n36\t| --- | --- |\n37\t| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n38\t| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n39\t| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n40\t| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n41\t| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n42\t| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n43\t| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n44\t| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n45\t| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n46\t| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n47\t| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n48\t| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n49\t| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n50\t\n51\tNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n52\t(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\n53\tengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n54\t\n55\t## Run, test, check\n56\t\n57\t```sh\n58\tnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\n59\tnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\n60\tnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\n61\tnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\n62\tnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\n63\tnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\n64\tnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\tOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\n68\tthe two scripts into the page.\n69\t\n70\t## Testing a game without a browser\n71\t\n72\t`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped.\n73\t\n74\t- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n75\t- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n76\t- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n77\t  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n78\t- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n79\t\n80\tThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n81\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"10fd43c2-a819-4540-9aa0-cc285e3b03f8","uuid":"7a8ad2fc-1e98-42f5-80a9-854977acb715","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:51:42.782Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.cache/opus55-plan-x17a627/engine/dist/handoff/README.md","content":"# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.85.2)\n\nEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\nfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n\n**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\nis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\nare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\nlacks.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n   `game/` and make it yours.\n3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n   before you use it.\n\n## What is here\n\n| File | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n\nNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\nengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n\n## Run, test, check\n\n```sh\nnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\nnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\nnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\nnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\nnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\nnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\nnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n```\n\nOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\nthe two scripts into the page.\n\n## Testing a game without a browser\n\n`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped.\n\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n\nThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n","numLines":81,"startLine":1,"totalLines":81}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011vsM72JRKUfj8xqcDh7mge","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggyKocByTbPPV2siGTSY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ke2TcT5CfoFetoCr983X7g","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.cache/opus55-plan-x17a627/engine/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":839,"cache_read_input_tokens":30153,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":839},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"10fd43c2-a819-4540-9aa0-cc285e3b03f8","uuid":"dec90111-e418-4c06-a305-066260115a63","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:51:43.197Z","request_id":"req_011CggyKoNoByzuGJm7nHnvt","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Ke2TcT5CfoFetoCr983X7g":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.cache/opus55-plan-x17a627/engine/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ke2TcT5CfoFetoCr983X7g","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n2\t\n3\tThis guide is for an AI (or a person) asked to build, port, remaster or remix a game with my-3D2dge. `API.md` is the compact reference, read whole: every single file carries it (a genre kit, `dist/my-3d2dge.html`, the compact one), and so does the handoff package. This guide explains the ideas, the workflow for remaking a classic, recipes for each genre, and the checks to run before handing a game back: search it for what you need. Its paths are the repository's (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); the handoff package's `README.md` says which of them it holds. Everything here matches `engine/my-3d2dge.js` v0.85.2.\n4\t\n5\tA coding agent that reads files gets the handoff package (`dist/handoff/`: the full engine readable, the cards, the tools, every 2D kit's game and a template game) or the agent edition alone (`dist/my-3d2dge-agent.js`: the essential engine in one readable file, its header, about 8k tokens, a complete manual with three example games). Games written for the agent edition run unchanged on the full engine, which adds the lighting, props, backdrops, touch controls and camera tools this guide also covers.\n6\t\n7\t## North star\n8\t\n9\tmy-3D2dge is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D engine built for AI models. It exists so that any model can:\n10\t\n11\t- port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras;\n12\t- make spiritual successors that feel like modern remasters, with better visuals, physics and controls;\n13\t- mash up mechanics across titles and genres.\n14\t\n15\tIt aims for **consistent quality**: the engine carries the hard parts (feel, physics, rendering, sound, UI), so each game only has to describe its rules and content.\n16\t\n17\t## The mental model\n18\t\n19\t1. **The world is 3D, the camera is 2D.** Game logic works in world units: x east, y south, z up, one tile = 16 units. A `View` projects world points to screen pixels. Switch the view and the same game renders as isometric, three-quarter, top-down, brawler, side-on or any custom angle.\n20\t2. **Characters are puppets, not sprite sheets.** A `Humanoid` is a 3D skeleton posed by math every step (IK legs and arms, gait from velocity, jumps, attacks, cape cloth). A `Blob` is a squashy sphere. Both project through the current view. Small objects can be string sprites (`E.sprite`).\n21\t3. **Genre kits.** Each kit handles one hard problem and knows nothing about genres, so they combine freely:\n22\t   - `TileMap` + `Body`: ground-plane levels and physics.\n23\t   - `PlatformMap` + `Platformer`: side-scrolling levels and a platformer controller.\n24\t   - `Bullets` + `E.pattern`: projectiles.\n25\t   - `Dialog` + `Menu`: text boxes and menus.\n26\t   - scenes: title, play, game over.\n27\t   - `game.audio`: chip sound.\n28\t\n29\t## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n30\t\n31\t1. **Name the core loop.** Mega Man: run, jump, shoot, reach the boss. Zelda: explore rooms, fight, find keys, open doors. 1942: dodge, shoot, survive waves, beat the boss. Write it as one sentence at the top of the game.\n32\t2. **Pick the view and the kit.** Use the genre playbook in `API.md`. A side-scroller is `PlatformMap` + `Platformer` in `'side'`; a top-down game is `TileMap` + `Body`. Pick a console resolution (`res: 'ps1'`, 320 x 240, or `'snes'`) so pixels are whole and the HUD's are known, and `expand: true` so the game fills any screen: the resolution is then the least it shows, and the HUD anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n33\t3. **Start from the closest kit.** Each genre has one, a complete small game with gameplay, HUD, win and lose, sound and touch controls: the playbook in `API.md` names it (in a single file its slice sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; in the handoff package it is `kits/<id>.js`). Change it into your game, with its own title and game-over scenes, and ship only that. Do not start from an empty file.\n34\t4. **Rebuild the content.** Levels are ASCII rows with a legend, enemies are small factories, items are sprites. Keep the original's layout ideas but make your own maps; for a remix, change the rules on purpose.\n35\t5. **Modernize.** The engine gives you most of it for free:\n36\t   - forgiving controls: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, input buffering;\n37\t   - hit feedback: hit-stop, shake, sparks, damage numbers;\n38\t   - smooth animation, lighting and glow;\n39\t   - a 2.5D view (`views: ['side', 'brawler']`);\n40\t   - pause, save data, touch controls.\n41\t6. **Tune the feel** with the feel numbers in `API.md`, then check the game with the skeleton overlay (`rig.debug(r)`) and in slow motion (`game.timeScale = .25`).\n42\t7. **Verify** with the checker and look at the screenshots (see the checklist at the end).\n43\t\n44\t## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n45\tPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n46\t\n47\t**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n48\t- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n49\t- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n50\t- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n51\t- *Bare rooms.* Put 3-8 props on every screen with `r.prop`: candles, chandeliers and stained-glass windows in castles; crates, barrels, signs and lamps in towns; trees, bushes, flowers, rocks and grass outdoors; crystals, bones, skulls, cobwebs and vines in caves; awnings, lanterns, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and fire drums on streets; sandbags, cactus and palms in deserts; mushrooms in forests. Tint props with `color` so they match the palette, and set `E.style.propSize = 1.4` when heroes are size 1.2+ so a door is taller than the hero.\n52\t- *Weak hits.* Every hit gets hit-stop (`game.freeze(.04-.08)`), shake, sparks and a sound. Every death and explosion gets `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` and, for big ones, `game.flash`. Pickups get `particles.glints`.\n53\t- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n54\t- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n55\t- *Muddy lighting.* Use lights for mood, not darkness: a few warm lights (`r.prop(..., { light: true })`) over an ambient of .4-.6; GPU lighting in top-down dungeons.\n56\t\n57\t**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n58\t- *Symphony of the Night:* `res: 'ps1'`, the `'castle-night'` backdrop, PlatformMap `stone` tiles with `moss` and `brick` walls, `'back'` tiles for the rear wall, candles and candelabras every few tiles, chandeliers, stained-glass `window` props, banners and statues. The hero is `build: 'heroic'`, `outfit: 'coat'`, `hair: 'long'`, `cape`, pale skin and silver hair, and the sword chops in the screen plane by itself in the side view; enemies are skeletons (`build: 'skeleton'`, add `outfit: 'robe'` and a staff for a lich), bats (`Blob` with `wings: true, mouth: 'fangs'`), cobwebs and vines on the walls.\n59\t- *Metal Slug:* the `'desert'` backdrop, `ground` and `metal` tiles, crates, barrels, sandbags, palms and cactus, `broken` pillars, `particles.explosion` everywhere (with `particles.ground` set so debris bounces on platforms), `bulky` soldiers with `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` while firing, and screen shake on every blast.\n60\t- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` walls under `tiles` roofs, `hedge` walls with `leaves` tops and `rock` cliffs with `grass` tops, trees, bushes, flowers, fences and mushrooms as props, `chibi` characters with `face: { eyes: 'big' }` and bright palettes, rabbites (`Blob` with `ears: 'rabbit', feet: true`), and dialog boxes with portraits.\n61\t- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +12), and a soft camera `lead`.\n62\t- *Final Fight / Streets of Rage:* the `brawler` view, a long street TileMap with brick and `plaster` building walls, awnings, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and burning fire drums along the sidewalk, `bulky` heroes and thugs with varied palettes, and big impacts.\n63\t\n64\t## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n65\tBroken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n66\t- **Moves, not raw specs.** `new E.Attack('overhead')`, `new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'])`. Every move in `E.MOVES` winds up, strikes with a lunge, step, lean and twist, follows through, holds, and eases back to the stance. It never sweeps backwards through its own arc.\n67\t- **A move set per character.** A knight gets `overhead`, `thrust` and `spin`; a mage `cast`; a rogue `jab`, `thrust` and `backslash`; a monk `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse` and `flyingkick`; a brute `haymaker`, `bash` and `sweep`; a beast `claw`. Two heroes must never share one swing.\n68\t- **Stances and poses between attacks.** Brawlers walk with `stance: 'guard'`, knights with `stance: 'ready'`. Win with `pose: 'cheer'`, cast with `'cast'`, block with `'block'` (weapons) or `'guard'` (fists), duck with `'crouch'`, taunt with `'hips'` or `'wave'`, fall with `down`, die with `'die'` (stagger, knees, topple), and climb ladders with `climb: true`. Faces act too: `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'`, and a Dialog portrait's mouth moves while its line types.\n69\t- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n70\t- **Check the motion.** `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game.html#scene --steps \"... rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records the swing frame by frame. Look for the wind-up, the strike and a clean settle. Add `--seed 1` for a repeatable strip, and `--compare before.html` (a copy of the page from before your change) to see exactly which pixels your change moved.\n71\t\n72\t## Hard rules\n73\t\n74\t1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n75\t2. **Draw only with engine primitives** (`E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props, `r.*` helpers). `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText`, canvas gradients and scaled `drawImage` anti-alias and break the pixel look. Translucency goes through the engine helpers (`px.blend`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc`, `r.shadow`): stepped alpha with blend modes by default (PS1 / N64 / SNES color math), or ordered dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for NES, Game Boy and Genesis looks.\n76\t3. **Anything that overlaps goes through the queue**: `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue`. Only backgrounds are drawn directly (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`, parallax shapes).\n77\t4. **Animate with continuous values** (phases, velocities, eased timers), never frame lists.\n78\t5. **Read input in `update`**: `pressed` for one-shot actions, `down` for held, `buffered` + `consume` for combat.\n79\t6. **A game is one script on the engine:** in a single file, the code between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; with files, `game.js` beside the `index.html` that loads the engine (inline both into the page to hand a person one file). **The engine is yours to change too.** Your copy (a clone of the repository, the handoff package, a single file) is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit. The readable engine is `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `engine/modules/` in the repository (`node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds every page from them), `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package (every page and test there loads it), and the script marked \"inlined from engine/my-3d2dge.js\" in `dist/my-3d2dge.html`; a genre kit's is minified. If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine lacks.\n80\t7. **Don't rebuild the standard features** (below): the pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen and pausing on focus loss come with every game. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`.\n81\t\n82\t## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n83\tEvery `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n84\t- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n85\t- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n86\t- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n87\t- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n88\t- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n89\t- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n90\t- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n91\t- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n92\t- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n93\t\n94\tAdjust them:\n95\t```js\n96\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n97\t  menu: {\n98\t    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n99\t    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n100\t      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n101\t    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n102\t    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n103\t  } });\n104\tconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\n105\tr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n106\t```\n107\t- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n108\t- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n109\t- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n110\t- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n111\t- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n112\t\n113\tWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n114\t- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n115\t- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n116\t- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n117\t- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n118\t- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n119\t\n120\t## Frame order\n121\t\n122\t`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n123\t\n124\t1. The clock adds the frame's real time (at most 50 ms) to what is left from the last frame, and runs one step for each whole 1/120 s of it: 2 steps a frame at 60 Hz, 1 at 120 Hz, 1 or 0 at 144 Hz. Each step ticks input, then (except during hit-stop and pause) runs the timers, the current scene's `update(dt)`, particles and the camera. The rest waits for the next frame: `game.alpha` (0..1) is how much of a step it holds, for interpolating drawing.\n125\t   - Slow motion (`game.timeScale = .3`) makes each step shorter (`dt = 1/120 * .3`), never fewer, so it stays smooth. Above 1 a step is split so none is longer than 1/120 s.\n126\t   - Paused, or at `timeScale` 0, the steps still run with `dt` 0, so input keeps ticking.\n127\t2. The renderer clears the buffer and positions the camera.\n128\t3. The scene's `draw(r)`. Draw backgrounds first, then register decals, queue items, actors, sprites, overlays and lights.\n129\t4. The renderer finishes the frame, in this order:\n130\t   1. particles and afterimages are queued;\n131\t   2. decals draw (ground marks, shadows, telegraphs);\n132\t   3. the queue sorts and draws by depth;\n133\t   4. lighting darkens the frame;\n134\t   5. overlays draw on top (HUD, dialog, menus, damage numbers);\n135\t   6. the frame is presented with a sub-pixel camera offset.\n136\t5. With GPU lighting active, every queue item is also drawn into a hidden info image, and WebGPU does the lighting and presentation.\n137\t\n138\tAn error in `update` or `draw` does not stop the loop. It is shown in a red box on screen and logged once to the console (`game.errors` keeps the list).\n139\t\n140\t## Systems in more depth\n141\t\n142\t### Views and resolution\n143\t- Presets: `iso` (yaw 45, pitch 30), `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (pitch 80), `brawler` (pitch 25), `side` (pitch 0).\n144\t- `overhead` (pitch 90, scale 1) makes world x/y equal screen pixels, for shooters, puzzle and arcade games. It is not in the default view cycle.\n145\t- Make your own with `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)`.\n146\t- `res` fixes the internal resolution with whole-number scaling. Without it, the size adapts to the window between `minH` and `maxW` × `maxH`; `portrait: { maxH }` lets the view grow on a screen held upright (a phone), so it fills the screen.\n147\t- `res` with `expand: true` (what the starter kits use) fills any window with no bars: `res` is the least the screen shows, and `r.W` × `r.H` grows on the long side (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427×240 of `'ps1'`, an ultrawide 640×270, a phone held upright 320×690). The game's job is what fills the extra space, edge to edge, with the HUD over the world's corners (never a band of nothing). The kits show the ways. A wider screen shows more level (the adventure's neighbouring rooms, dimmed a little: they wait) or widens the play area itself (the shooter's waves spread across the whole screen). A taller one shows more world (the forest round the adventure's rooms, the RPG's sky darkening to stars), zooms a side view in (the platformer, the brawler and the RPG's battle at 1.5x, a scene's `camera.tall`, the battle's two sides closing up to fit) and lifts the level onto a band below it (the platformer's soil, the brawler's foreground wall). A painted backdrop is painted at the screen's size and zoom, its ground meeting the floor's edge. Keep prompts clear of `game.input.touchArea()` and HUDs clear of `game.cornerArea()` (the menu button stays at the top right on a phone). On a phone, make the controls smart: a button there only when it has something to do (`input.touchSet`: TALK beside someone), taps on what you see (the RPG's commands and foes), a thumb that steers from anywhere on the screen (`input.steer = 'drag'`, `input.drag()`: the shooter's ship goes 1.5x as far, so one swipe crosses it), what a phone player shouldn't have to hold done for them (the shooter fires by itself), buttons a thumb hits (44 CSS px and more: the RPG's battle pads, with AUTO), and a camera that never shows ground a fighter can't walk to (the brawler stops at a fight's lock). And keep the frame a phone's: a 120 Hz screen gives 8.3 ms, and frames that miss it (their cost, or the GPU behind them) are drawn every other refresh (`game.drawEvery`), so draw background characters at half rate (`r.actor(..., { every: 2, key })`: the adventure's townsfolk) and leave GPU lighting to a computer (its main pointer fine). Settings, Display, Show frame rate reads it on the device, and Frame rate lets the player fix it (60: every other refresh of a 120 Hz screen; Full: every refresh). `npm run test:phone` checks the kits on an emulated phone.\n148\t- `game.views` limits view cycling (`game.nextView()`) and tells the checker which views to test.\n149\t\n150\t### Scenes\n151\tA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n152\t- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n153\t- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n154\t\n155\t### Camera\n156\t- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n157\t- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n158\t- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n159\t- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n160\t- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n161\t- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n162\t\n163\t### Players (two to four on one machine)\n164\t- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n165\t- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n166\t- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n167\t- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n168\t\n169\t### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n170\tThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n171\t- **Tweens** (Phaser's tweens, Unity's DOTween): `game.tween(target, { x: 10, y: 4 }, seconds, { ease, delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases a target's numbers to new values, from where they are when its delay ends; a door sliding, a title dropping in, a coin flying to the HUD, a camera move. `ease` is a name in `E.ease` (Penner's: `linear`, `inSine`, `outBack`, `outElastic`, `outBounce`, ...; `outQuad` by default) or a function of 0..1. It replaces a timer, a start value and a lerp in every update.\n172\t- **Scripts** (Unity's coroutines): `game.script(function* (game) { ... })` runs a generator in the update step and waits at each `yield`: a number of seconds, a condition (`E.until(fn)` or a function), a tween, another script or a generator (run and waited for), `E.all([...])`, `E.any([...])`, or nothing (one step). A wave timeline is a loop (`for (const w of WAVES) { spawn(w); yield E.any([w.time, () => !enemies.length]); }`); a cutscene is its lines in order (`talk.say(...)`, `yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`, `yield game.tween(cam, ...)`), instead of a state machine with a timer per state. It runs at once to its first `yield`; `.cancel()` stops it and runs its `finally`.\n173\t- **Paths:** `new E.Path(points, { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })` measures a line by distance along it: `at(d)` is a point with its heading (a moving platform's place, an enemy's dive, a rail, a camera track: `d = speed * time`), `nearest(x, y)` the distance along of the nearest point (a racer's progress round the lap, for places), `draw(r)` shows it while you build.\n174\t- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind, amount, wind, lightning }` (rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand): drops fall through the world's height in ground views and in three layers of parallax in side and overhead views, splash or settle on the floor (`particles.ground`, the platforms' height in a side view), and lightning flashes (the player's Screen flashes setting) and thunders. It is cosmetic: its own seeded random stream, never `Math.random`, so replays and gameplay are untouched. `E.WEATHER` holds the kinds; add your own.\n175\t- **Sprites** (frames, turns, swaps): `E.anim([...frames], fps, { loop, pingpong })` (or one sheet cut into `frames`) is played by `r.sprite(x, y, z, anim, { t })`; `rot` turns a sprite in whole pixels, cached in 16 or 32 steps as the era's games pre-rotated theirs (a ship facing its heading, a spinning blade); `scale` grows it nearest-neighbour; `E.recolor(spr, map)` is a palette swap (the second player, a stronger enemy).\n176\t- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n177\t\n178\t### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n179\t- ASCII `rows` and a `legend`: a number is a wall type, a string is a spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn }` combines them.\n180\t- Wall `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH }`. `cut` lowers a wall when it stands between the camera and the floor.\n181\t- `floorTex(x, y, floorTag)` colors the floor per pixel; it is baked once per view. Ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`.\n182\t- `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` opens doors or breaks walls; the floor and wall caches rebuild themselves.\n183\t- Collision is height-aware: `collide(body)` ignores walls lower than `body.z + step`, and `groundAt(x, y, r, z)` returns the surface to stand on.\n184\t- Things standing on a block sort after it, and `r.shadow(..., surfaceZ)` draws on top of blocks.\n185\t- `map.block(cx, cy, on)` and `map.set(...)` bump `map.version`; a `FlowField` rebuilds at its next `update` when it changes.\n186\t- Maps built in code: `new E.TileMap({ w, h, types })` starts as open floor; `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)` puts a block of wall type `id` in (0 clears it), and `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)` tags a floor cell (`'grass'`, `'water'`) and blocks it or not.\n187\t- Facades come from wall-type options: `windows: { w, h, step, from, lit }` (or `true`), `door: { chance, color }`, `shutters: color`, `balcony: { z, color }`, `arch: { chance }`. For anything else, `types[id].decorate(face)` paints each wall face as it is baked (so it costs nothing per frame): `face.side`, `face.u0..u1` along the wall in world units (patterns line up across cells), `face.z0..z1`, `face.at(u, z)`, `face.quad(u0, z0, u1, z1, color)`, `face.line`, `face.dot`, `face.tones`, `face.view`.\n188\t- A big thing over several blocks (a canopy over a tower, a cart across two cells) sorts by its whole base: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { footprint: [x0, y0, x1, y1] })` lifts it over every block under it. `r.prop` and `r.sprite` take `footprint` too.\n189\t\n190\t### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n191\tOne rule for collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing, so a game with roofs never writes its own:\n192\t- World units, one tile = 16, z up. A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor, and a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h` (one column per cell: no passages under things yet).\n193\t- A body stands on the highest top within `z + step` under its footprint (`step` 2, `Body.step`), so a kerb of 2 is walked over and a block of 14 is jumped onto.\n194\t- Eyes are at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) and a target is seen at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22), the heights of a size-1.3 Humanoid. A body's own `eye` and `chest` override them: a crouching hero sets `hero.chest = 8`.\n195\t- \"The same level\" is `|dz| <= E.HEIGHTS.level` (8) unless a call says otherwise. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.inArc` is flat unless you pass `dz`, and `E.overlap` is always flat (pair it with `E.sameLevel`).\n196\t\n197\tThe queries on a `TileMap` (`dir` is an angle in radians or a ground vector `[dx, dy]`; `from` is a body or any `{ x, y, z, r }`):\n198\t- `map.ray(a, b, { eye, target })` walks every cell between two points and returns `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, where it stops. `map.sight(a, b)` is eyes to chest; `los()` only looks across the ground (a 12-unit planter blocks it, not `sight`).\n199\t- `map.standable(x, y, z, r)`: room to stand, held up, not in deep water.\n200\t- `map.ledge(from, dir, { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 })` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge: [x, y] }`: the nearest top to pull up onto, and where the hands grip.\n201\t- `map.landing(from, dir, { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 })` → `{ x, y, z, d }`: a spot across a gap that a jump arc clears.\n202\t- `map.drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`: what lies past the edge ahead (hop-downs, fall warnings, guards that won't walk off roofs).\n203\t- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`: a parabola from feet to feet, peaking `apex` above the higher end, clears every top (jumps, throws, aerial takedowns).\n204\t\n205\tReach and perception:\n206\t- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n207\t- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` and `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol)`.\n208\t\n209\tNavigation: `new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 })` walks the tops a body can stand on. Next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most `step`, or by up to `climb` going up and `drop` going down. A guard on a roof gets a field across the roofs; with `climb` and `drop` set, it chases between levels. With `jump` (the widest gap, world units) it also links roofs across a gap a jump clears, and a climb costs more than a leap, so a chase leaps rather than drop to the street and climb back. `flow.next(x, y)` → `{ x, y, z, kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge }` says where to head: an AI on the ground presses jump for a `climb` or a `jump` once `edge` (the distance to the side of its cell it leaves by) is under its radius plus a little, and `E.Mover` does the climb or the leap.\n210\t\n211\t```js\n212\tconst seen = map.sight(guard, hero) && E.inArc(guard, guard.facing, hero, 120, .7);   // eyes over planters, not through roofs\n213\tif (input.pressed('jump')) {\n214\t  const up = map.ledge(hero, hero.facing);   // a wall to climb in front?\n215\t  const across = !up && map.landing(hero, hero.facing);   // or a gap to clear?\n216\t  if (up) climbTo(up); else if (across) leapTo(across); else hero.jump(210);\n217\t}\n218\tconst prey = E.targets(hero, guards, { range: 22, arc: 1.2, facing: hero.facing, sight: map })[0];   // the same level by default\n219\tif (prey && input.pressed('attack')) takedown(prey.target);\n220\t```\n221\t\n222\t### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n223\t- Tiles live on the x/z plane; the ASCII top row is the highest.\n224\t- Kinds:\n225\t  - `solid`;\n226\t  - `slope`: `dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left, `from` / `to` (0..1) for gentle slopes; bodies walk up and down them and step up onto low ledges;\n227\t  - `oneway`: you stand on it and jump up through it, and down + jump drops through;\n228\t  - `ladder`: climbable, and you can stand on its top;\n229\t  - `hazard`: not solid; test it with `touching(body, 'hazard')`;\n230\t  - `deco`: drawn in the play plane;\n231\t  - `back`: a darker background wall.\n232\t- Styles give each tile its look: `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (grass top when open to the sky, with `flower`), `block` (bevelled), `bonus` (`?` block, `glyph` option), `pipe`, `plank`, `spikes`, `ladder`, `liquid`, `plain`; `mat` gives a solid tile's face any material (`E.MATERIALS`).\n233\t- In `side` the level is flat like an NES or SNES game. In a tilted view with yaw 0 (`brawler` or custom), tiles show top faces and the level becomes a 2.5D diorama.\n234\t- `move(body, dt, solids)` moves any box `{ x, z, vx, vz, w, h }` with sub-steps. It reports:\n235\t  - `onGround` and `onOneWay`;\n236\t  - `hitWall`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 none;\n237\t  - `hitCeiling`;\n238\t  - `bumped`: the tile hit from below;\n239\t  - `ground`: the moving solid it stands on.\n240\t- `solids` are extra boxes (moving platforms, elevators, crates); a body standing on one is carried by its `vx` / `vz`.\n241\t\n242\t### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n243\t- Defaults feel like a modern NES remaster: run 95, jump 285 (about 3 tiles), gravity 800 rising and 1150 falling, fall cap 330.\n244\t- Forgiving controls: coyote time 0.09 s, jump buffer 0.13 s, jump cut 0.45 when jump is released early.\n245\t- Optional moves: `airJumps` (double jump), `wallJump` (with wall slide), `dash`, `climb` speed.\n246\t- `update(dt, level, input | intent, solids)`. The same controller runs enemies: pass `{ x, jump, jumpPressed }` from your AI.\n247\t- `rigState(extra)` returns the object `Humanoid.update` wants, including the airborne pose.\n248\t\n249\t### Body (ground-plane physics)\n250\t- Velocity with optional `friction` (per second) and `bounce` (off walls and the floor).\n251\t- `push(ix, iy, iz)` adds knockback; `jump(v)` jumps with `gravity`.\n252\t- It lands on the highest walkable surface: the floor, or the top of a block low enough to step onto.\n253\t- Use it for heroes, enemies, bombs, crates, pickups and balls.\n254\t\n255\t### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n256\t`E.Mover` is the ground views' answer to `Platformer`: a `Body` with a third-person game's movement.\n257\t- `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y, z })` (the index files its members as `mover.`); each step `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` with `input: 'THIRDPERSON'` (move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch, J attack, K block, L dodge; touch buttons), or an AI's intent `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`. Screen directions turn into ground ones through the view.\n258\t- Walk 50, run 85, sprint 125 and sneak 32 with acceleration; a jump with coyote time and a buffer, once a press.\n259\t- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n260\t- Climbing any wall (`climbAny: true`, Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild): a jump press at a wall too tall for a ledge latches on, on the ground or in the air (`mover.wall` is the face, `map.wall(from, dir)`'s: its point, normal, top and facing). The stick climbs as the screen shows it (up the screen is up the wall; an AI's intent: `move: [along, -up]`), it turns outer and inner corners, a jump press leaps up (`climbJump`, which ends on the wall) or, pulled away, pushes off; near the top it pulls up (`climbUp`), at the floor it stands, crouch lets go. Lower `climbMax` (30) so roofs are climbed, not jumped onto. The STEALTH slice and the template do this.\n261\t- Gliding (`glide: true`, Breath of the Wild, Arkham): a jump press in the air, `glideMin` (24) over the ground, opens a sail: it sinks at `glideSink`, flies at `glideSpeed`, takes no fall damage, rises over an updraft (`lift`: a speed, or `m => inUpdraft(m) ? 40 : 0`), and closes on a press, on landing, or at a wall (a climber takes hold). `mover.gliding` says so; the rig hangs under it with its hands up, so draw the sail over them (the template does).\n262\t- Stamina (off until you set `staminaMax`; the template's is 100): climbing, climb jumps, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina` (`tire`), standing refills it (`staminaRegen`); at 0 it lets go of the wall, the glide closes and it can't sprint (`mover.exhausted`) until a third is back. Show it with `ui.hud({ bars: [{ label: 'Stamina', value: mover.stamina, max: mover.staminaMax }] })`.\n263\t- Falls: past `rollAt` (26) at speed it rolls, past `hurtAt` (72) it sets `hurt`. `lastSafe` and `respawn()` bring it back from water and pits (`inHazard`).\n264\t- `mover.rigState(extra)` feeds `rig.update`: its crouch (a sneaking gait), sprint lean, air, landing, or the playing action's pose.\n265\t- Actions (`E.ACTIONS`): `climbUp` (played backwards to climb down), `vault`, `hopDown`, `roll` (invulnerable in its middle), `getUp`, and `takedown`, a paired action that moves two bodies: `mover.play('takedown', { other: guard, aerial, map })`. While `mover.action` plays it moves the body instead of physics; `act.beat`, `act.u`, `act.canCancel`, `act.invulnerable`, `act.otherState()` for the victim's rig. Your own: `E.ACTIONS.slide = { beats: [['go', .4]], setup(A) {}, at(A, beat, u) { return { x, y, z, state } } }`.\n266\t\n267\t### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n268\t- `E.sees(guard, hero, { map, range: 120, fov: 1.6, eye })` → `{ seen, d, angle, exposure }`: inside the cone, and three points on the hero (feet, chest, head) checked against every top with `map.ray`. Crouching shows less and lower; `hero.exposure` (0..1) is cover you set: tall grass, shadow.\n269\t- `E.hears(guard, { x, y, z, loud: 80 }, map)`: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius, halved through a wall.\n270\t- `const mind = new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget })`; each step `mind.update(dt, E.sees(...), hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'` (it rises faster the nearer and more exposed the hero is), `mind.last` (where it last saw or heard), `mind.changed`. An alert guard that loses the hero searches for `forget` seconds; `mind.hear(strength, at)` notices a noise.\n271\t- Drawing: `r.sightCone(guard, { map, range, fov, color, alpha })` (cut short by walls) and `r.marker(x, y, z, { label, color })`, an objective marker that becomes an arrow at the screen's edge.\n272\t\n273\t### Attack (melee timing)\n274\t- `new E.Attack(spec)` owns the wind-up, active and recover timers of one move; the spec is also the Humanoid attack spec.\n275\t- `start()` begins a swing (pass `true` to chain from recover into a combo); `update(dt)` returns the phase that just began, which is where swing sounds go.\n276\t- `hits(targets, test, fn)` calls `fn` once per target per swing while active. `state` feeds `rig.update({ attack })`.\n277\t- `E.Combo([spec1, spec2, spec3], { window })` chains attacks: `press()` flows into the next hit during recover (or shortly after), otherwise restarts. `E.knockback(from, target, speed, up)` pushes the target away.\n278\t- **Special moves** (input sequences): `game.input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing })` is true on the step a fireball's motion ends: down, down-forward, forward, then the button (numpad notation, as fighting games write moves: `'623'` a dragon punch, `'41236'` a half circle, `'66'` a dash), mirrored when the fighter faces left; the button may land with the last direction, and the whole move must come within `window` seconds (.45). `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })` reads any run of inputs (a cheat code, a rhythm pattern); a step joins actions with `+`. Both read `input.history`, the last second of directions (numpad digits) and presses, which an input display can draw.\n279\t\n280\t### Bullets and patterns\n281\t- A pool on the `'ground'` plane (top-down) or the `'side'` plane.\n282\t- Bullets die on walls with a small spark; `hit(targets, fn, team)` tests circles (side targets use their box center).\n283\t- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n284\t- Bullets glow under GPU lighting. Set `sprite` for custom art and `pierce` to pass through targets.\n285\t\n286\t### Humanoid\n287\t- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n288\t- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n289\t- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n290\t- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n291\t- Attacks:\n292\t  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n293\t  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n294\t  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n295\t- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n296\t\n297\t### Audio\n298\t- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n299\t- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n300\t- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n301\t- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n302\t- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n303\t- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n304\t\n305\t### Text and UI\n306\t- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"10fd43c2-a819-4540-9aa0-cc285e3b03f8","uuid":"49c01b8a-136e-4a30-aa3a-664f07f303d7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:51:43.348Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.cache/opus55-plan-x17a627/engine/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","content":"# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n\nThis guide is for an AI (or a person) asked to build, port, remaster or remix a game with my-3D2dge. `API.md` is the compact reference, read whole: every single file carries it (a genre kit, `dist/my-3d2dge.html`, the compact one), and so does the handoff package. This guide explains the ideas, the workflow for remaking a classic, recipes for each genre, and the checks to run before handing a game back: search it for what you need. Its paths are the repository's (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); the handoff package's `README.md` says which of them it holds. Everything here matches `engine/my-3d2dge.js` v0.85.2.\n\nA coding agent that reads files gets the handoff package (`dist/handoff/`: the full engine readable, the cards, the tools, every 2D kit's game and a template game) or the agent edition alone (`dist/my-3d2dge-agent.js`: the essential engine in one readable file, its header, about 8k tokens, a complete manual with three example games). Games written for the agent edition run unchanged on the full engine, which adds the lighting, props, backdrops, touch controls and camera tools this guide also covers.\n\n## North star\n\nmy-3D2dge is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D engine built for AI models. It exists so that any model can:\n\n- port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras;\n- make spiritual successors that feel like modern remasters, with better visuals, physics and controls;\n- mash up mechanics across titles and genres.\n\nIt aims for **consistent quality**: the engine carries the hard parts (feel, physics, rendering, sound, UI), so each game only has to describe its rules and content.\n\n## The mental model\n\n1. **The world is 3D, the camera is 2D.** Game logic works in world units: x east, y south, z up, one tile = 16 units. A `View` projects world points to screen pixels. Switch the view and the same game renders as isometric, three-quarter, top-down, brawler, side-on or any custom angle.\n2. **Characters are puppets, not sprite sheets.** A `Humanoid` is a 3D skeleton posed by math every step (IK legs and arms, gait from velocity, jumps, attacks, cape cloth). A `Blob` is a squashy sphere. Both project through the current view. Small objects can be string sprites (`E.sprite`).\n3. **Genre kits.** Each kit handles one hard problem and knows nothing about genres, so they combine freely:\n   - `TileMap` + `Body`: ground-plane levels and physics.\n   - `PlatformMap` + `Platformer`: side-scrolling levels and a platformer controller.\n   - `Bullets` + `E.pattern`: projectiles.\n   - `Dialog` + `Menu`: text boxes and menus.\n   - scenes: title, play, game over.\n   - `game.audio`: chip sound.\n\n## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n\n1. **Name the core loop.** Mega Man: run, jump, shoot, reach the boss. Zelda: explore rooms, fight, find keys, open doors. 1942: dodge, shoot, survive waves, beat the boss. Write it as one sentence at the top of the game.\n2. **Pick the view and the kit.** Use the genre playbook in `API.md`. A side-scroller is `PlatformMap` + `Platformer` in `'side'`; a top-down game is `TileMap` + `Body`. Pick a console resolution (`res: 'ps1'`, 320 x 240, or `'snes'`) so pixels are whole and the HUD's are known, and `expand: true` so the game fills any screen: the resolution is then the least it shows, and the HUD anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n3. **Start from the closest kit.** Each genre has one, a complete small game with gameplay, HUD, win and lose, sound and touch controls: the playbook in `API.md` names it (in a single file its slice sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; in the handoff package it is `kits/<id>.js`). Change it into your game, with its own title and game-over scenes, and ship only that. Do not start from an empty file.\n4. **Rebuild the content.** Levels are ASCII rows with a legend, enemies are small factories, items are sprites. Keep the original's layout ideas but make your own maps; for a remix, change the rules on purpose.\n5. **Modernize.** The engine gives you most of it for free:\n   - forgiving controls: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, input buffering;\n   - hit feedback: hit-stop, shake, sparks, damage numbers;\n   - smooth animation, lighting and glow;\n   - a 2.5D view (`views: ['side', 'brawler']`);\n   - pause, save data, touch controls.\n6. **Tune the feel** with the feel numbers in `API.md`, then check the game with the skeleton overlay (`rig.debug(r)`) and in slow motion (`game.timeScale = .25`).\n7. **Verify** with the checker and look at the screenshots (see the checklist at the end).\n\n## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\nPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n\n**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n- *Bare rooms.* Put 3-8 props on every screen with `r.prop`: candles, chandeliers and stained-glass windows in castles; crates, barrels, signs and lamps in towns; trees, bushes, flowers, rocks and grass outdoors; crystals, bones, skulls, cobwebs and vines in caves; awnings, lanterns, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and fire drums on streets; sandbags, cactus and palms in deserts; mushrooms in forests. Tint props with `color` so they match the palette, and set `E.style.propSize = 1.4` when heroes are size 1.2+ so a door is taller than the hero.\n- *Weak hits.* Every hit gets hit-stop (`game.freeze(.04-.08)`), shake, sparks and a sound. Every death and explosion gets `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` and, for big ones, `game.flash`. Pickups get `particles.glints`.\n- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n- *Muddy lighting.* Use lights for mood, not darkness: a few warm lights (`r.prop(..., { light: true })`) over an ambient of .4-.6; GPU lighting in top-down dungeons.\n\n**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n- *Symphony of the Night:* `res: 'ps1'`, the `'castle-night'` backdrop, PlatformMap `stone` tiles with `moss` and `brick` walls, `'back'` tiles for the rear wall, candles and candelabras every few tiles, chandeliers, stained-glass `window` props, banners and statues. The hero is `build: 'heroic'`, `outfit: 'coat'`, `hair: 'long'`, `cape`, pale skin and silver hair, and the sword chops in the screen plane by itself in the side view; enemies are skeletons (`build: 'skeleton'`, add `outfit: 'robe'` and a staff for a lich), bats (`Blob` with `wings: true, mouth: 'fangs'`), cobwebs and vines on the walls.\n- *Metal Slug:* the `'desert'` backdrop, `ground` and `metal` tiles, crates, barrels, sandbags, palms and cactus, `broken` pillars, `particles.explosion` everywhere (with `particles.ground` set so debris bounces on platforms), `bulky` soldiers with `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` while firing, and screen shake on every blast.\n- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` walls under `tiles` roofs, `hedge` walls with `leaves` tops and `rock` cliffs with `grass` tops, trees, bushes, flowers, fences and mushrooms as props, `chibi` characters with `face: { eyes: 'big' }` and bright palettes, rabbites (`Blob` with `ears: 'rabbit', feet: true`), and dialog boxes with portraits.\n- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +12), and a soft camera `lead`.\n- *Final Fight / Streets of Rage:* the `brawler` view, a long street TileMap with brick and `plaster` building walls, awnings, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and burning fire drums along the sidewalk, `bulky` heroes and thugs with varied palettes, and big impacts.\n\n## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\nBroken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n- **Moves, not raw specs.** `new E.Attack('overhead')`, `new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'])`. Every move in `E.MOVES` winds up, strikes with a lunge, step, lean and twist, follows through, holds, and eases back to the stance. It never sweeps backwards through its own arc.\n- **A move set per character.** A knight gets `overhead`, `thrust` and `spin`; a mage `cast`; a rogue `jab`, `thrust` and `backslash`; a monk `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse` and `flyingkick`; a brute `haymaker`, `bash` and `sweep`; a beast `claw`. Two heroes must never share one swing.\n- **Stances and poses between attacks.** Brawlers walk with `stance: 'guard'`, knights with `stance: 'ready'`. Win with `pose: 'cheer'`, cast with `'cast'`, block with `'block'` (weapons) or `'guard'` (fists), duck with `'crouch'`, taunt with `'hips'` or `'wave'`, fall with `down`, die with `'die'` (stagger, knees, topple), and climb ladders with `climb: true`. Faces act too: `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'`, and a Dialog portrait's mouth moves while its line types.\n- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n- **Check the motion.** `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game.html#scene --steps \"... rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records the swing frame by frame. Look for the wind-up, the strike and a clean settle. Add `--seed 1` for a repeatable strip, and `--compare before.html` (a copy of the page from before your change) to see exactly which pixels your change moved.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n2. **Draw only with engine primitives** (`E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props, `r.*` helpers). `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText`, canvas gradients and scaled `drawImage` anti-alias and break the pixel look. Translucency goes through the engine helpers (`px.blend`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc`, `r.shadow`): stepped alpha with blend modes by default (PS1 / N64 / SNES color math), or ordered dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for NES, Game Boy and Genesis looks.\n3. **Anything that overlaps goes through the queue**: `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue`. Only backgrounds are drawn directly (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`, parallax shapes).\n4. **Animate with continuous values** (phases, velocities, eased timers), never frame lists.\n5. **Read input in `update`**: `pressed` for one-shot actions, `down` for held, `buffered` + `consume` for combat.\n6. **A game is one script on the engine:** in a single file, the code between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; with files, `game.js` beside the `index.html` that loads the engine (inline both into the page to hand a person one file). **The engine is yours to change too.** Your copy (a clone of the repository, the handoff package, a single file) is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit. The readable engine is `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `engine/modules/` in the repository (`node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds every page from them), `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package (every page and test there loads it), and the script marked \"inlined from engine/my-3d2dge.js\" in `dist/my-3d2dge.html`; a genre kit's is minified. If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine lacks.\n7. **Don't rebuild the standard features** (below): the pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen and pausing on focus loss come with every game. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`.\n\n## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\nEvery `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n\nAdjust them:\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n  menu: {\n    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n  } });\nconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\nr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n```\n- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n\nWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n\n## Frame order\n\n`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n\n1. The clock adds the frame's real time (at most 50 ms) to what is left from the last frame, and runs one step for each whole 1/120 s of it: 2 steps a frame at 60 Hz, 1 at 120 Hz, 1 or 0 at 144 Hz. Each step ticks input, then (except during hit-stop and pause) runs the timers, the current scene's `update(dt)`, particles and the camera. The rest waits for the next frame: `game.alpha` (0..1) is how much of a step it holds, for interpolating drawing.\n   - Slow motion (`game.timeScale = .3`) makes each step shorter (`dt = 1/120 * .3`), never fewer, so it stays smooth. Above 1 a step is split so none is longer than 1/120 s.\n   - Paused, or at `timeScale` 0, the steps still run with `dt` 0, so input keeps ticking.\n2. The renderer clears the buffer and positions the camera.\n3. The scene's `draw(r)`. Draw backgrounds first, then register decals, queue items, actors, sprites, overlays and lights.\n4. The renderer finishes the frame, in this order:\n   1. particles and afterimages are queued;\n   2. decals draw (ground marks, shadows, telegraphs);\n   3. the queue sorts and draws by depth;\n   4. lighting darkens the frame;\n   5. overlays draw on top (HUD, dialog, menus, damage numbers);\n   6. the frame is presented with a sub-pixel camera offset.\n5. With GPU lighting active, every queue item is also drawn into a hidden info image, and WebGPU does the lighting and presentation.\n\nAn error in `update` or `draw` does not stop the loop. It is shown in a red box on screen and logged once to the console (`game.errors` keeps the list).\n\n## Systems in more depth\n\n### Views and resolution\n- Presets: `iso` (yaw 45, pitch 30), `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (pitch 80), `brawler` (pitch 25), `side` (pitch 0).\n- `overhead` (pitch 90, scale 1) makes world x/y equal screen pixels, for shooters, puzzle and arcade games. It is not in the default view cycle.\n- Make your own with `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)`.\n- `res` fixes the internal resolution with whole-number scaling. Without it, the size adapts to the window between `minH` and `maxW` × `maxH`; `portrait: { maxH }` lets the view grow on a screen held upright (a phone), so it fills the screen.\n- `res` with `expand: true` (what the starter kits use) fills any window with no bars: `res` is the least the screen shows, and `r.W` × `r.H` grows on the long side (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427×240 of `'ps1'`, an ultrawide 640×270, a phone held upright 320×690). The game's job is what fills the extra space, edge to edge, with the HUD over the world's corners (never a band of nothing). The kits show the ways. A wider screen shows more level (the adventure's neighbouring rooms, dimmed a little: they wait) or widens the play area itself (the shooter's waves spread across the whole screen). A taller one shows more world (the forest round the adventure's rooms, the RPG's sky darkening to stars), zooms a side view in (the platformer, the brawler and the RPG's battle at 1.5x, a scene's `camera.tall`, the battle's two sides closing up to fit) and lifts the level onto a band below it (the platformer's soil, the brawler's foreground wall). A painted backdrop is painted at the screen's size and zoom, its ground meeting the floor's edge. Keep prompts clear of `game.input.touchArea()` and HUDs clear of `game.cornerArea()` (the menu button stays at the top right on a phone). On a phone, make the controls smart: a button there only when it has something to do (`input.touchSet`: TALK beside someone), taps on what you see (the RPG's commands and foes), a thumb that steers from anywhere on the screen (`input.steer = 'drag'`, `input.drag()`: the shooter's ship goes 1.5x as far, so one swipe crosses it), what a phone player shouldn't have to hold done for them (the shooter fires by itself), buttons a thumb hits (44 CSS px and more: the RPG's battle pads, with AUTO), and a camera that never shows ground a fighter can't walk to (the brawler stops at a fight's lock). And keep the frame a phone's: a 120 Hz screen gives 8.3 ms, and frames that miss it (their cost, or the GPU behind them) are drawn every other refresh (`game.drawEvery`), so draw background characters at half rate (`r.actor(..., { every: 2, key })`: the adventure's townsfolk) and leave GPU lighting to a computer (its main pointer fine). Settings, Display, Show frame rate reads it on the device, and Frame rate lets the player fix it (60: every other refresh of a 120 Hz screen; Full: every refresh). `npm run test:phone` checks the kits on an emulated phone.\n- `game.views` limits view cycling (`game.nextView()`) and tells the checker which views to test.\n\n### Scenes\nA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n\n### Camera\n- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n\n### Players (two to four on one machine)\n- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n\n### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\nThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n- **Tweens** (Phaser's tweens, Unity's DOTween): `game.tween(target, { x: 10, y: 4 }, seconds, { ease, delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases a target's numbers to new values, from where they are when its delay ends; a door sliding, a title dropping in, a coin flying to the HUD, a camera move. `ease` is a name in `E.ease` (Penner's: `linear`, `inSine`, `outBack`, `outElastic`, `outBounce`, ...; `outQuad` by default) or a function of 0..1. It replaces a timer, a start value and a lerp in every update.\n- **Scripts** (Unity's coroutines): `game.script(function* (game) { ... })` runs a generator in the update step and waits at each `yield`: a number of seconds, a condition (`E.until(fn)` or a function), a tween, another script or a generator (run and waited for), `E.all([...])`, `E.any([...])`, or nothing (one step). A wave timeline is a loop (`for (const w of WAVES) { spawn(w); yield E.any([w.time, () => !enemies.length]); }`); a cutscene is its lines in order (`talk.say(...)`, `yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`, `yield game.tween(cam, ...)`), instead of a state machine with a timer per state. It runs at once to its first `yield`; `.cancel()` stops it and runs its `finally`.\n- **Paths:** `new E.Path(points, { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })` measures a line by distance along it: `at(d)` is a point with its heading (a moving platform's place, an enemy's dive, a rail, a camera track: `d = speed * time`), `nearest(x, y)` the distance along of the nearest point (a racer's progress round the lap, for places), `draw(r)` shows it while you build.\n- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind, amount, wind, lightning }` (rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand): drops fall through the world's height in ground views and in three layers of parallax in side and overhead views, splash or settle on the floor (`particles.ground`, the platforms' height in a side view), and lightning flashes (the player's Screen flashes setting) and thunders. It is cosmetic: its own seeded random stream, never `Math.random`, so replays and gameplay are untouched. `E.WEATHER` holds the kinds; add your own.\n- **Sprites** (frames, turns, swaps): `E.anim([...frames], fps, { loop, pingpong })` (or one sheet cut into `frames`) is played by `r.sprite(x, y, z, anim, { t })`; `rot` turns a sprite in whole pixels, cached in 16 or 32 steps as the era's games pre-rotated theirs (a ship facing its heading, a spinning blade); `scale` grows it nearest-neighbour; `E.recolor(spr, map)` is a palette swap (the second player, a stronger enemy).\n- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n\n### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n- ASCII `rows` and a `legend`: a number is a wall type, a string is a spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn }` combines them.\n- Wall `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH }`. `cut` lowers a wall when it stands between the camera and the floor.\n- `floorTex(x, y, floorTag)` colors the floor per pixel; it is baked once per view. Ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`.\n- `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` opens doors or breaks walls; the floor and wall caches rebuild themselves.\n- Collision is height-aware: `collide(body)` ignores walls lower than `body.z + step`, and `groundAt(x, y, r, z)` returns the surface to stand on.\n- Things standing on a block sort after it, and `r.shadow(..., surfaceZ)` draws on top of blocks.\n- `map.block(cx, cy, on)` and `map.set(...)` bump `map.version`; a `FlowField` rebuilds at its next `update` when it changes.\n- Maps built in code: `new E.TileMap({ w, h, types })` starts as open floor; `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)` puts a block of wall type `id` in (0 clears it), and `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)` tags a floor cell (`'grass'`, `'water'`) and blocks it or not.\n- Facades come from wall-type options: `windows: { w, h, step, from, lit }` (or `true`), `door: { chance, color }`, `shutters: color`, `balcony: { z, color }`, `arch: { chance }`. For anything else, `types[id].decorate(face)` paints each wall face as it is baked (so it costs nothing per frame): `face.side`, `face.u0..u1` along the wall in world units (patterns line up across cells), `face.z0..z1`, `face.at(u, z)`, `face.quad(u0, z0, u1, z1, color)`, `face.line`, `face.dot`, `face.tones`, `face.view`.\n- A big thing over several blocks (a canopy over a tower, a cart across two cells) sorts by its whole base: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { footprint: [x0, y0, x1, y1] })` lifts it over every block under it. `r.prop` and `r.sprite` take `footprint` too.\n\n### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\nOne rule for collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing, so a game with roofs never writes its own:\n- World units, one tile = 16, z up. A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor, and a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h` (one column per cell: no passages under things yet).\n- A body stands on the highest top within `z + step` under its footprint (`step` 2, `Body.step`), so a kerb of 2 is walked over and a block of 14 is jumped onto.\n- Eyes are at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) and a target is seen at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22), the heights of a size-1.3 Humanoid. A body's own `eye` and `chest` override them: a crouching hero sets `hero.chest = 8`.\n- \"The same level\" is `|dz| <= E.HEIGHTS.level` (8) unless a call says otherwise. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.inArc` is flat unless you pass `dz`, and `E.overlap` is always flat (pair it with `E.sameLevel`).\n\nThe queries on a `TileMap` (`dir` is an angle in radians or a ground vector `[dx, dy]`; `from` is a body or any `{ x, y, z, r }`):\n- `map.ray(a, b, { eye, target })` walks every cell between two points and returns `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, where it stops. `map.sight(a, b)` is eyes to chest; `los()` only looks across the ground (a 12-unit planter blocks it, not `sight`).\n- `map.standable(x, y, z, r)`: room to stand, held up, not in deep water.\n- `map.ledge(from, dir, { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 })` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge: [x, y] }`: the nearest top to pull up onto, and where the hands grip.\n- `map.landing(from, dir, { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 })` → `{ x, y, z, d }`: a spot across a gap that a jump arc clears.\n- `map.drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`: what lies past the edge ahead (hop-downs, fall warnings, guards that won't walk off roofs).\n- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`: a parabola from feet to feet, peaking `apex` above the higher end, clears every top (jumps, throws, aerial takedowns).\n\nReach and perception:\n- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` and `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol)`.\n\nNavigation: `new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 })` walks the tops a body can stand on. Next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most `step`, or by up to `climb` going up and `drop` going down. A guard on a roof gets a field across the roofs; with `climb` and `drop` set, it chases between levels. With `jump` (the widest gap, world units) it also links roofs across a gap a jump clears, and a climb costs more than a leap, so a chase leaps rather than drop to the street and climb back. `flow.next(x, y)` → `{ x, y, z, kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge }` says where to head: an AI on the ground presses jump for a `climb` or a `jump` once `edge` (the distance to the side of its cell it leaves by) is under its radius plus a little, and `E.Mover` does the climb or the leap.\n\n```js\nconst seen = map.sight(guard, hero) && E.inArc(guard, guard.facing, hero, 120, .7);   // eyes over planters, not through roofs\nif (input.pressed('jump')) {\n  const up = map.ledge(hero, hero.facing);   // a wall to climb in front?\n  const across = !up && map.landing(hero, hero.facing);   // or a gap to clear?\n  if (up) climbTo(up); else if (across) leapTo(across); else hero.jump(210);\n}\nconst prey = E.targets(hero, guards, { range: 22, arc: 1.2, facing: hero.facing, sight: map })[0];   // the same level by default\nif (prey && input.pressed('attack')) takedown(prey.target);\n```\n\n### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n- Tiles live on the x/z plane; the ASCII top row is the highest.\n- Kinds:\n  - `solid`;\n  - `slope`: `dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left, `from` / `to` (0..1) for gentle slopes; bodies walk up and down them and step up onto low ledges;\n  - `oneway`: you stand on it and jump up through it, and down + jump drops through;\n  - `ladder`: climbable, and you can stand on its top;\n  - `hazard`: not solid; test it with `touching(body, 'hazard')`;\n  - `deco`: drawn in the play plane;\n  - `back`: a darker background wall.\n- Styles give each tile its look: `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (grass top when open to the sky, with `flower`), `block` (bevelled), `bonus` (`?` block, `glyph` option), `pipe`, `plank`, `spikes`, `ladder`, `liquid`, `plain`; `mat` gives a solid tile's face any material (`E.MATERIALS`).\n- In `side` the level is flat like an NES or SNES game. In a tilted view with yaw 0 (`brawler` or custom), tiles show top faces and the level becomes a 2.5D diorama.\n- `move(body, dt, solids)` moves any box `{ x, z, vx, vz, w, h }` with sub-steps. It reports:\n  - `onGround` and `onOneWay`;\n  - `hitWall`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 none;\n  - `hitCeiling`;\n  - `bumped`: the tile hit from below;\n  - `ground`: the moving solid it stands on.\n- `solids` are extra boxes (moving platforms, elevators, crates); a body standing on one is carried by its `vx` / `vz`.\n\n### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n- Defaults feel like a modern NES remaster: run 95, jump 285 (about 3 tiles), gravity 800 rising and 1150 falling, fall cap 330.\n- Forgiving controls: coyote time 0.09 s, jump buffer 0.13 s, jump cut 0.45 when jump is released early.\n- Optional moves: `airJumps` (double jump), `wallJump` (with wall slide), `dash`, `climb` speed.\n- `update(dt, level, input | intent, solids)`. The same controller runs enemies: pass `{ x, jump, jumpPressed }` from your AI.\n- `rigState(extra)` returns the object `Humanoid.update` wants, including the airborne pose.\n\n### Body (ground-plane physics)\n- Velocity with optional `friction` (per second) and `bounce` (off walls and the floor).\n- `push(ix, iy, iz)` adds knockback; `jump(v)` jumps with `gravity`.\n- It lands on the highest walkable surface: the floor, or the top of a block low enough to step onto.\n- Use it for heroes, enemies, bombs, crates, pickups and balls.\n\n### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n`E.Mover` is the ground views' answer to `Platformer`: a `Body` with a third-person game's movement.\n- `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y, z })` (the index files its members as `mover.`); each step `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` with `input: 'THIRDPERSON'` (move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch, J attack, K block, L dodge; touch buttons), or an AI's intent `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`. Screen directions turn into ground ones through the view.\n- Walk 50, run 85, sprint 125 and sneak 32 with acceleration; a jump with coyote time and a buffer, once a press.\n- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n- Climbing any wall (`climbAny: true`, Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild): a jump press at a wall too tall for a ledge latches on, on the ground or in the air (`mover.wall` is the face, `map.wall(from, dir)`'s: its point, normal, top and facing). The stick climbs as the screen shows it (up the screen is up the wall; an AI's intent: `move: [along, -up]`), it turns outer and inner corners, a jump press leaps up (`climbJump`, which ends on the wall) or, pulled away, pushes off; near the top it pulls up (`climbUp`), at the floor it stands, crouch lets go. Lower `climbMax` (30) so roofs are climbed, not jumped onto. The STEALTH slice and the template do this.\n- Gliding (`glide: true`, Breath of the Wild, Arkham): a jump press in the air, `glideMin` (24) over the ground, opens a sail: it sinks at `glideSink`, flies at `glideSpeed`, takes no fall damage, rises over an updraft (`lift`: a speed, or `m => inUpdraft(m) ? 40 : 0`), and closes on a press, on landing, or at a wall (a climber takes hold). `mover.gliding` says so; the rig hangs under it with its hands up, so draw the sail over them (the template does).\n- Stamina (off until you set `staminaMax`; the template's is 100): climbing, climb jumps, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina` (`tire`), standing refills it (`staminaRegen`); at 0 it lets go of the wall, the glide closes and it can't sprint (`mover.exhausted`) until a third is back. Show it with `ui.hud({ bars: [{ label: 'Stamina', value: mover.stamina, max: mover.staminaMax }] })`.\n- Falls: past `rollAt` (26) at speed it rolls, past `hurtAt` (72) it sets `hurt`. `lastSafe` and `respawn()` bring it back from water and pits (`inHazard`).\n- `mover.rigState(extra)` feeds `rig.update`: its crouch (a sneaking gait), sprint lean, air, landing, or the playing action's pose.\n- Actions (`E.ACTIONS`): `climbUp` (played backwards to climb down), `vault`, `hopDown`, `roll` (invulnerable in its middle), `getUp`, and `takedown`, a paired action that moves two bodies: `mover.play('takedown', { other: guard, aerial, map })`. While `mover.action` plays it moves the body instead of physics; `act.beat`, `act.u`, `act.canCancel`, `act.invulnerable`, `act.otherState()` for the victim's rig. Your own: `E.ACTIONS.slide = { beats: [['go', .4]], setup(A) {}, at(A, beat, u) { return { x, y, z, state } } }`.\n\n### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n- `E.sees(guard, hero, { map, range: 120, fov: 1.6, eye })` → `{ seen, d, angle, exposure }`: inside the cone, and three points on the hero (feet, chest, head) checked against every top with `map.ray`. Crouching shows less and lower; `hero.exposure` (0..1) is cover you set: tall grass, shadow.\n- `E.hears(guard, { x, y, z, loud: 80 }, map)`: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius, halved through a wall.\n- `const mind = new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget })`; each step `mind.update(dt, E.sees(...), hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'` (it rises faster the nearer and more exposed the hero is), `mind.last` (where it last saw or heard), `mind.changed`. An alert guard that loses the hero searches for `forget` seconds; `mind.hear(strength, at)` notices a noise.\n- Drawing: `r.sightCone(guard, { map, range, fov, color, alpha })` (cut short by walls) and `r.marker(x, y, z, { label, color })`, an objective marker that becomes an arrow at the screen's edge.\n\n### Attack (melee timing)\n- `new E.Attack(spec)` owns the wind-up, active and recover timers of one move; the spec is also the Humanoid attack spec.\n- `start()` begins a swing (pass `true` to chain from recover into a combo); `update(dt)` returns the phase that just began, which is where swing sounds go.\n- `hits(targets, test, fn)` calls `fn` once per target per swing while active. `state` feeds `rig.update({ attack })`.\n- `E.Combo([spec1, spec2, spec3], { window })` chains attacks: `press()` flows into the next hit during recover (or shortly after), otherwise restarts. `E.knockback(from, target, speed, up)` pushes the target away.\n- **Special moves** (input sequences): `game.input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing })` is true on the step a fireball's motion ends: down, down-forward, forward, then the button (numpad notation, as fighting games write moves: `'623'` a dragon punch, `'41236'` a half circle, `'66'` a dash), mirrored when the fighter faces left; the button may land with the last direction, and the whole move must come within `window` seconds (.45). `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })` reads any run of inputs (a cheat code, a rhythm pattern); a step joins actions with `+`. Both read `input.history`, the last second of directions (numpad digits) and presses, which an input display can draw.\n\n### Bullets and patterns\n- A pool on the `'ground'` plane (top-down) or the `'side'` plane.\n- Bullets die on walls with a small spark; `hit(targets, fn, team)` tests circles (side targets use their box center).\n- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n- Bullets glow under GPU lighting. Set `sprite` for custom art and `pierce` to pass through targets.\n\n### Humanoid\n- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n- Attacks:\n  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n\n### Audio\n- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n\n### Text and UI\n- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos.","numLines":306,"startLine":1,"totalLines":665,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Ke2TcT5CfoFetoCr983X7g","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
